     Predictable.  But could you keep the ad hominem tripe in talk. and
alt. groups where it belongs (at least slightly)?
     Now, since some of the points you brought up were quite good:

:1) In a real dungeon, you would never be in such a hurry that you would
:   step into a visible and obvious danger, like a pond on the screen is.
:   If you do, you are playing too fast.
     Actually, I've never personally stepped in a pond, although I have
hit a floating eye (which have a habit of suddenly appearing in
passageways) several times.  Less often now that I use the shift-<keys>
more often, but those keys can't often be used and since ctrl-<key> is
unavailable on an IBM interface and g-<key> is too inconvenient and
prone to klutzy fingers, it still happens every so often.

:2) The need to look around for danger is realistic; not doing so and
:   suffering the consequences, _however_lethal_, is not evidence for
:   an argument that the game is unplayable.
     However, the issue here is not a failure to look around so much as
a failure to properly input ones actions.  While this appears initially
to be the fault of the player, it is more so a fault of the playing
conditions.  In short, Nethack is not a game of skill.
     One plus, I might note, of the IBM interface is that pools *look
like* pools, and are blue, so it's easy enough to avoid them.  I can't
say the same for Unix workstations that operate slowly.

:3) For every feature of Nethack that kills a character stone cold
:   irretrievably dead in the midst of a great game, there is a detailed
:   and incontrovertible argument that that feature is _grossly_ unfair,
:   unplayable, unrealistic, buggy, or all four.
     If you say so.  I say that some issues are different than others.

:4) The result of the union of all those arguments is a game in which
:   no character ever dies without being first politely asked if it is
:   OK, and every game results in a character being a total winner.
     True.  If you represent this issue as black-and-white, the
the appropriate conclusion is to remove all confirmations period. 
However, there is this thing called "gray [not grey] space"

:5) Everyone knows the line to stop that kind of reasoning has to be
:   drawn somewhere, or the game becomes the equivalent of a D&D
:   Monty Haul campaign.
     Congratulations!  You've just brought us back to square one, i.e.
"do we draw the line here or elsewhere?"  I say we draw it elsewhere, so
as to make Nethack more a strategy-oriented, rather than skill-oriented,
game.  Fortunately, you address the point in your forthcoming point: 

:6) No one wants the line drawn until after the feature s/he copes with
:   worst is removed, ameliorated, modified, or otherwise gutted.
     Actually, I'd rather see Nethack's IBM interface switch the
functions of the shift- and ctrl-keys, rewrite the ctrl-key interface so
as to stop less often, and then have the fountain interface reworked to
avoid so much randomness in a game function which is, although not
necessary in all cases, necessary in some.  Apart from fountains, pools
only appear in predictable places, so there shouldn't be a problem after
that.  (That's just my bias against a game that includes features based
on chance that can't be influenced to a significant degree by proper
strategy.)  I don't advocate changing much else, although I wouldn't
mind seeing a "monster confirm:" option and a "pool confirm" option. 
Neither of these would make it easier to develop a proper strategy; both
would help eliminate the element of skill in a game that does not run in
an environment that promotes use of skill.

:7) Somebody has to have the guts to take their medicine, shut up, and
:   go back and try to win the game _as_it_is_!

:8) I elect you, because I'm tired of listening to all this bellyaching.
     If you'd prefer that people "shut up" and don't want to listen to
"all this bellyaching," you can always unsubscribe to the newsgroup and
pretend.

:Have I made myself perfectly clear?
     Absolutely.  That doesn't, as you seem to assert by accusing me of
"missing the point," make you perfectly right.

:Kent, the man from xanth.
:<xanthian@Zorch.SF-Bay.ORG> <xanthian@well.sf.ca.us>
:
:[Who has never won a game of hack or nethack, probably never will, and
:likes it just fine that way, thank you!  Easy games are for sissies;
:that's why I play tourists by preference.  I have been getting
:asymptotically closer to winning for years without ever doing so, and
:that's fine too.  A won game loses its interest immediately, and I
:_like_ nethack.]
     Believe it or not, I like nethack as well.  I have won earlier
versions of the game, and didn't find them fun (they had an ugly
tendency to have the character grow in power exponentially and the
monsters linearly, and compensated this only by starting the character
out below the level of the monsters).  I also think that any game has
room for improvement.  I don't think that the improvements mentioned
here make the game *easier* as far as strategy is concerned; they simply
take out the element of skill involved.
